TEXT 32 432 Left 2 ;Transformer max. current = 10A RMS
TEXT 536 128 Left 2 ;An open delta connection transformer uses two single-phase\ntransformer to supply a three phase load. Also known as a V-V\nsystem, this setup is typically used only in emergencies \n(one transformer in revision or disconnected for repair)\nbecause it is less efficient than a closed delta. (factor 1.73205)\nThis combination can supply a maximum of 17.32kVA\nwhereas each transformer operates at its rated capacity\nat 10KVA
TEXT 216 184 Left 2 ;See Q is flowing in circle\nfrom trafo1 to trafo2 and \nreturn (only inside)
TEXT 152 512 Left 2 ;This is a normal transformer with 3x1 transormers\noperating at nominal power
TEXT 168 40 Left 2 ;10<0°A
TEXT 240 152 Left 2 ;10<-120°A
TEXT 160 264 Left 2 ;10<120°A
TEXT 360 120 Left 2 ;1k<30°V
TEXT 408 200 Left 2 ;1k<90°V
TEXT 536 224 Left 2 ;From picture above you can see that\nFrom Transformer1 S=U*conj(I)=1000<30° * conj(-10<0°) = -8660.2-j5000 \n(Transformer1 delivers active power 8660W and also delivers\n reactive power 5000VAr to the second transformer)\nFrom Transformer2 S=U*conj(I)=1000<90° * conj(-10<120°) = -8660.2+j5000\n(Transformer2 delivers active power to port2 and consumes\nreactive power from transformer1\nYou can see that active load of both transformers is 8660W and\nand there is an exchange of reacitve power between transformer1\nand transformer2 !!\nDue to this reason total kVA rating of transformers\nis more than kVA rating of the load !!!!!\nSqrt(8660*8660+5000*5000)=10kVA is the rating of the transformers
TEXT -72 96 Left 1 ;NOTE reactive power\nflow positive in the\ndirection of the \nsmall arrow symbol
